{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a strong downtrend (e.g., Example 1) but sentiment remains flat or neutral, the model systematically overestimates future prices by failing to account for immediate mean reversion; apply a downward correction of 5-10% to the forecasted path.",
    "When history exhibits high volatility with sharp spikes followed by consolidation (e.g., Examples 2 and 3), the model fails to capture the 'overshoot' effect where prices exceed recent highs before correcting; reduce the forecasted trajectory by approximately 3-5 EUR per day during the first week of the horizon.",
    "In periods of mixed sentiment with moderate volatility (e.g., Example 4), the model exhibits a lag bias, predicting slower recovery than actual market dynamics; increase the slope of the forecast curve by 10-15% when recent highs are not yet confirmed as new peaks.",
    "When sentiment history shows intermittent positive spikes amidst neutral or negative trends (e.g., Examples 4 and 5), the model underestimates short-term volatility and over-smooths the path; introduce a higher variance band around the central forecast line to account for sudden reversals."
  ]
}
